      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no single definitive \"yes\" or \"no\" because the ROI depends heavily on how you define the expenses and what \"overhead\" you are measuring; current consensus generally shows that the time and legal costs inherent in DEI are significantly outweighed by gains in innovation and retention over the long term.\n\nTo address their concerns directly in the memo, you should frame the \"overhead\" argument by citing that the initial administrative costs are one-time implementation efforts, whereas the ROI materializes as a recurring \"innovation dividend\" or risk mitigation benefit. If you need a concrete metric to counter the \"distraction\" claim, provide a side-by-side comparison of a known \"best-in-class\" and \"laggard\" firm in the same industry, clearly showing which one outperformed the other on both human capital and revenue growth.",
